    <string name="isp_driver_note_title">About the CH340 driver</string>
    <string name="isp_driver_note">Android has no kernel driver for CH340/CH341, and none is needed: this tool ships drivers for the common USB-to-serial chips (CH340, CH341, CP2102, FT232/FT2232, PL2303, standard CDC).\n\nIf no device is found, check in order:\n1. the phone supports and enables OTG (some models need USB permission in developer options);\n2. the adapter is powered — the status bar should show "USB device detected";\n3. the chip VID/PID is not in the built-in driver list — use a CH340/CP2102 adapter instead;\n4. "Deny" was tapped in the system dialog — unplug, reconnect and allow it.</string>
    <string name="isp_help_text">Purpose: download .bin/.hex firmware into the STM32 internal flash over a USB-to-serial adapter (CH340 etc.), using the serial ISP protocol of the bootloader burned into the chip (8 data bits / even parity / 1 stop bit).\n\nWiring: cross TX/RX between the adapter and the chip UART, GND must be connected. To enter the bootloader automatically you also need DTR→BOOT0 and RTS→NRST (many breakout adapters expose these pins).\n\nSteps:\n1. Plug in the adapter → rescan → connect;\n2. choose the bootloader entry method (default 1: RTS reset + DTR for BOOT0; pick 0 manual if those two lines are not wired);\n3. select firmware: .hex carries its own addresses, .bin needs the "BIN start address" field (usually 08000000);\n4. tap "Start download". The log shows sync, erase, write and verify step by step.\n\nNote: downloading performs a full chip erase, so the program already in the chip is removed — use "Read back flash" in the menu first if you want a backup. Removing read protection also erases everything.</string>

